---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Data Migration Strategies: From Spreadsheets to Databases" description="A guide to migrating data from Excel and CSV files into relational databases, NoSQL stores, and cloud data warehouses." keywords="data migration, spreadsheet to database, excel to database, csv import database" breadcrumbs={[{name:'Home',url:'/'},{name:'Blog',url:'/blog'},{name:'Data Migration Strategies',url:'/blog/data-migration-strategies'}]}>
  <main class="blog-post">
    <article>
      <h1>Data Migration Strategies: From Spreadsheets to Databases</h1>
      <p class="post-meta"><time datetime="2026-02-28">February 28, 2026</time></p>

      <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504639725590-34d0984388bd?w=800&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop" alt="Data server with blinking lights" class="post-img" loading="lazy" />

      <p>Every growing business reaches a point where spreadsheets are no longer enough. Customer lists, inventory records, and financial data outgrow Excel's limits. Moving that data into a proper database is a critical step — but migration is fraught with pitfalls. Here is a structured approach.</p>

      <h2>Step 1: Audit the Source Data</h2>
      <p>Before writing any import script, understand what you are working with. Open the spreadsheet and check for merged cells, inconsistent column types, empty rows, and non-standard date formats. Document every column: its name, data type, allowed values, and whether it can be null. This audit becomes your schema blueprint.</p>

      <h2>Step 2: Design the Target Schema</h2>
      <p>Spreadsheets are flat; databases are normalized. A single spreadsheet with columns for customer name, order date, and product name may need to become three tables: <code>customers</code>, <code>products</code>, and <code>orders</code>. Identify primary keys, foreign key relationships, and constraints before importing. Resist the temptation to import the spreadsheet as a single table — that defeats the purpose of using a database.</p>

      <h2>Step 3: Convert to CSV</h2>
      <p>CSV is the universal import format for databases. Most database systems (PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, SQL Server) have built-in commands for bulk CSV import: <code>COPY</code>, <code>LOAD DATA INFILE</code>, or <code>BULK INSERT</code>. Convert each sheet to CSV using a tool like this one. Ensure the CSV output uses UTF-8 encoding and consistent quoting.</p>

      <h2>Step 4: Import and Validate</h2>
      <p>Import the CSV into a staging table (a temporary copy of the target table structure). This lets you run validation queries before moving data into the production schema. Check for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rows that failed to import (type mismatches, constraint violations)</li>
        <li>Duplicate records that need to be merged</li>
        <li>Null values in columns that should not be null</li>
        <li>Referential integrity — foreign key values that do not exist in parent tables</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 5: Transform and Load</h2>
      <p>Once the staging data passes validation, run your transformation queries: normalize flat columns into related tables, cast types, generate surrogate keys, and apply business rules. Then insert the clean data into the production tables. This is best done as a transaction — if anything fails, roll back the entire operation.</p>

      <h2>Step 6: Verify and Decommission</h2>
      <p>After migration, run reconciliation queries. Compare record counts, sum numeric columns, and spot-check individual records between the source spreadsheet and the database. Once you are confident the data matches, archive the spreadsheet and update your data sources to point to the database.</p>

      <h2>Common Pitfalls</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Encoding issues:</strong> Excel files may use Windows-1252 encoding. Convert to UTF-8 before importing.</li>
        <li><strong>Large files:</strong> Spreadsheets over 100 MB may need chunked import. Split the CSV into 10,000-row batches.</li>
        <li><strong>Date formats:</strong> Excel serial dates (like 45000) need conversion to ISO 8601. Handle this during the transform step.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Summary</h2>
      <p>Spreadsheet-to-database migration is a multi-step process that rewards careful planning. Audit first, design the schema, convert to CSV, validate in staging, then load. Skip any step and you will be debugging data issues for weeks.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

